    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's go through it line by line. Max asks Vika a playful question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "А я кто?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what am I?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'who' and builds toward 'who'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A croissant or celery?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'a croissant' directly with 'celery'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, of course a croissant.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'of course' directly with 'a croissant', combining 'of course' with 'a croissant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Почему круассан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why a croissant?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'why' directly with 'a croissant', combining 'why' with 'a croissant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Я же не ем мучное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't eat flour products.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'to eat' to negate the action. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты спас наш ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You saved our restaurant.' Vika uses the perfective verb meaning 'saved' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'restaurant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты молодец, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You did great, Max.' Vika uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'Max', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "max",
      "t": "Я просто сделал свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I just did my job.' Max expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'just' with 'did' and 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, это было круто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, it was cool.' Vika chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'was' for the ongoing action, followed by 'cool'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты очень помог мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You helped me a lot.' Vika uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'to me', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you.' Vika delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'thank you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мой храбрый круассан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My brave croissant.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'brave' straight to 'a croissant'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
